Compile-time analyzers shipped inside packages

Meridian.Mapping also ships Meridian.Mapping.Generators, and
Meridian.Mediator.Generators ships its own generator assembly, as compile-time
analyzers (not runtime dependencies of consuming applications). In each of these
packages the analyzers/dotnet/cs folder holds only that compiled Meridian
generator assembly; the packages also contain the Meridian LICENSE and README
(and, for Meridian.Mapping, its runtime assemblies). No third-party assembly is
embedded in or copied into any Meridian package. eng/pack-packages.ps1 fails a
package whose analyzer folder or nuspec dependency set differs from the
inventory. A check that compares every packed file list with the private
build-tooling closure below is not implemented yet.

Build-time tooling that is NOT distributed

The two generator projects are built against private build-time packages
(PrivateAssets=all): Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp 4.8.0,
Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers 5.3.0 and NETStandard.Library 2.0.3 with their
transitive closure. They are restored on the machine that builds a package and
are neither dependencies of, nor files inside, any Meridian package. The pack
evidence records them separately for every candidate (scope
private-analyzer-build).

Six packages in that private closure carry no modern nuspec license field, only
a legacy license URL, so their license is recorded from the license text
embedded in each package archive rather than inferred:
- NETStandard.Library 2.0.3, System.Buffers 4.5.1, System.Memory 4.5.5,
  System.Numerics.Vectors 4.4.0 and System.Threading.Tasks.Extensions 4.5.4:
  the embedded text is the MIT License.
- Microsoft.NETCore.Platforms 1.1.0: the embedded text is the "MICROSOFT
  SOFTWARE LICENSE TERMS, MICROSOFT .NET LIBRARY" agreement. These are NOT the
  MIT License. The package is a runtime-identifier catalog (runtime.json) with
  no assembly. It is used only while building the generators and nothing from it
  is included in any Meridian package. Anyone who redistributes a build
  environment, container image or restored package cache that contains this
  package takes on its terms separately; Meridian does not relicense it.

Archive and license-text SHA-256 values and the full disposition are recorded in
docs/package-compatibility.md ("Dependency licenses and build-time tooling").

